<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph Stalin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Iosif Vissarionovich Stalin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Stalin (1878–1953) was the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from the late 1920s until his death. A Georgian Bolshevik, he rose through the post of general secretary of the Communist Party after Lenin's death and imposed forced collectivisation, rapid industrialisation and mass terror that cost millions of lives, including the famine of 1932–1933. He led the Soviet Union to victory over Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and extended its control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin.jpg" alt="Joseph Stalin" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franklin D. Roosevelt Library Public Domain Photographs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StalinCropped1943.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The khanate is the historical homeland state of the Crimean Tatars, whose national identity, language and memory of statehood remain tied to it, especially after their mass deportation by <b>Stalin</b>’s government in 1944 and their return from the late 1980s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1928 <b>Joseph Stalin</b>’s five-year plans transformed the republic, which held most of the Soviet Union’s industry and resources: new industrial centres such as Magnitogorsk rose in the Urals and Siberia, cities grew rapidly and literacy became almost universal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>To regulate relations between these nominally sovereign republics, the Bolshevik leaders debated their form: <b>Joseph Stalin</b>, People's Commissar for Nationalities, proposed absorbing them into the Russian republic as autonomous units, while Lenin insisted on a federation of formally equal republics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bolsheviks seized power in Russia in 1917 and created the Soviet Union, where <b>Joseph Stalin</b> had established personal and totalitarian rule by 1929; Benito Mussolini came to power in Italy in 1922 and from 1925 built the first fascist dictatorship; and Adolf Hitler, appointed chancellor in January…</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Lenin and his successors argued that the working class needed a single, disciplined vanguard to take power and then to defend it: “the proletariat needs the Party first of all as its General Staff”, <b>Joseph Stalin</b> wrote in The Foundations of Leninism (1924).</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>East Germany's Lied der Partei, whose title line held that the party is always right, named <b>Stalin</b> in its chorus until his name was removed in the 1960s.</blockquote><a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gori, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. Died in Kuntsevo Dacha, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.98,44.11&amp;year=1926" aria-label="Gori on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5622 669 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Joseph Stalin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6072 939h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6072 939h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.98,44.11&amp;year=1926">What was here in 1926? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
